GS bands: the rows of a banded run are split into slices that the threads take in turn. The rows are split into PS2X_GS_BAND_SLICES slices per band thread (default 3) of about the same estimated cost, and the worker and the band threads each take the next slice until none is left, drawing it with their band context begun anew; rows that turn out slower than their estimate then hold one thread for a slice instead of the whole run. Every slice with rows replays all of the run's CLUT loads, so the worker's context after its last slice holds the CLUT state the run leaves behind (without a slice it replays them alone). Checked in Metropolismania: PS2X_GS_BANDS_VERIFY=1 over 18,400 banded runs, no difference from drawing them serially; all 4 MB of VRAM byte-identical at the eleven views of a scripted city camera run with frame skip off; PS2Recomp's tests give the same results. Explore autoplay with frame skip off: the six threads' busy time per 2 s went from 1,237 to 1,470 ms (one band each) to 1,385 to 1,403 ms, 42.9 to 43.5 fps (lowest window 33.2 to 34.5); 6 slices per thread balance better still but cost more than they save.

